      <title>US forces accused of ‘weaponising’ Somalia’s floods by trapping women and children in the path of El Niño</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/26/us-forces-accused-of-weaponising-somalia-flood-el-nino</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Exclusive:&lt;/strong&gt; Coordinated airstrikes on territory held by al-Shabaab are driving the civilian population to find refuge alongside a river where rising water levels are a threat to safety. Is it a deliberate military strategy?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The water was rising rapidly. It had already reached Cayni Ali’s waist. Her home, five metres from the flooded River Juba in southern Somalia, never stood a chance. There was nowhere to go. Ali’s city of Jilib was under attack. Repeated airstrikes, said the mother of six, were pinning its population beside the rising river.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;“The drones come all the time,” said the 27-year-old. “We hear them constantly. They kill animals, civilians. The town is big but destruction is everywhere.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/26/us-forces-accused-of-weaponising-somalia-flood-el-nino"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
